# TEMPLATE. Copy to ~/.config/maddi/internal-names.txt and put the real terms there.
#
# The real list is deliberately NOT in this repository, and must never be added
# to it. maddi is public, and a list of the names to redact is a decoder ring for
# the redaction: anyone reading it learns exactly what was removed, which undoes
# the removal. Keeping it outside is the whole point.
#
# The scanner (scan-internal-names.pl) looks for the list in this order:
#
#   1. $MADDI_INTERNAL_NAMES
#   2. git config maddi.internalNames
#   3. ~/.config/maddi/internal-names.txt
#
# With no list present it prints a notice and passes: an outside contributor
# cannot leak names they were never given. Configured-but-missing is an error.
#
# FORMAT: one Perl-compatible regular expression per line, matched
# case-insensitively. Blank lines and lines starting with '#' are ignored.
#
# WRITE EACH PATTERN SO IT DOES NOT MATCH ITSELF. Use a one-character class for
# one letter: `f[o]o` matches "foo", but the line you are reading does not
# contain "foo". Without that, the list reports itself on every scan.
#
# Wrap a pattern in (?-i: ) to make it case-sensitive — that is what stops a
# short prefix from matching ordinary words. Prefer broad patterns without word
# boundaries: a name also appears inside camel case and package segments, which
# a boundary would miss. False positives are cheap to allow for; a leak is not.
#
# Examples only — these match nothing real:

a[c]mecorp
w[i]dgetworks(?!hop)
(?-i:A[c]m[A-Z])
